{
  "id": "oob-012",
  "pattern": "off-by-one",
  "difficulty_rating": 1500,
  "explanation": "The loop condition `while (low < high)` should be `while (low <= high)`. Standard binary search must still check the element when the search space has narrowed to exactly one index (`low === high`) — that index is still a valid, unexamined candidate. With `low < high`, the loop exits the instant `low` catches up to `high` without ever comparing `arr[low]` (or `arr[high]`) to `target`. This silently drops the last remaining candidate from consideration. Tracing target=8: low/high narrow from (0,7)→(0,2)→(2,2), and arr[2]=8 is never checked, so it wrongly returns -1 instead of 2. target=23 happens to get matched mid-loop (before low===high), so it correctly returns 5. target=45 narrows to (7,7) without ever checking arr[7]=45, wrongly returning -1 instead of 7. target=99 (genuinely absent) also narrows to (7,7) unchecked, but since it isn't in the array anyway, -1 is coincidentally the right answer for the wrong reason.",
  "prompt": "Step through each binarySearch call and predict what gets printed.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function binarySearch(arr, target) {\n  let low = 0;\n  let high = arr.length - 1;\n  while (low < high) {\n    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);\n    if (arr[mid] === target) {\n      return mid;\n    } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n      low = mid + 1;\n    } else {\n      high = mid - 1;\n    }\n  }\n  return -1;\n}\n\nconst sorted = [2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 45];\n\nconsole.log(binarySearch(sorted, 8));\nconsole.log(binarySearch(sorted, 23));\nconsole.log(binarySearch(sorted, 45));\nconsole.log(binarySearch(sorted, 99));",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
